        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Leonard Jimmie Savage, Milton Friedman, Edwin Hewitt\n- Roles: economist, mathematician\n- Concepts: Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences, Hewitt–Savage zero–one law, abstract harmonic analysis\n\nFacts:\n- Milton Friedman was an American economist born on July 31, 1912, and he died on November 16, 2006.\n- Edwin Hewitt was an American mathematician born on January 20, 1920, in Everett, Washington, and he died on June 21, 1999.\n- Edwin Hewitt was known for work in abstract harmonic analysis.\n- Edwin Hewitt and Leonard Jimmie Savage discovered the Hewitt–Savage zero–one law.\n- Milton Friedman received the 1976 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for research on consumption analysis, monetary history and theory, and the complexity of stabilization policy.\n- Milton Friedman said Leonard Jimmie Savage was \"one of the few people I have met whom I would unhesitatingly call a genius.\"",
